Reviewed `src/blog/people/drafts/Mel-Robbins.md` (297 lines) against the research file and a light web check. Fresh-eyes notes are appended at the bottom of the draft.

**The two biggest issues:**

1. **The ending doesn't know it has a payoff sitting right there.** The whole closing section is built on her sign-off — "In case anyone hasn't told you today, I love you" — and her media company is named **143 Studios**, after Mister Rogers' 1-4-3 code for exactly that phrase. A Type 3 who *incorporated* the sentence nobody said to her at 41 is the single strongest fact available on this subject, and the draft ends one inch short of it.

2. **The 2019 daytime talk show is missing entirely.** Canceled after one season, 0.4 household rating, ~615K viewers — less than half its competition. The draft's arc runs 2008 collapse → 2015 diagnosis → podcast dominance with no professional failure in between. For a profile whose thesis is "the confession is the product," leaving out her most visible public failure is the strangest gap in the piece — and it's the cleanest available test of that thesis.

Two more worth surfacing: the critic pressure aims at a soft target ("her advice sounds dumb" is a charge she publicly agrees with, so the section is a layup) while the live clinical critique — that *Let Them* rebrands DBT radical acceptance with thin credit — goes unmentioned, even though it's the same "Threes absorb" mechanism the draft already argues, at much larger scale. And "I hate my husband" runs in the hook, the TL;DR, and the pot roast quote, then Chris Robbins disappears; his public depression disclosure came through a video *she* posted, which is her confessional habit applied to someone else's private life.

I made no body edits, per the command.

Sources: [Sony Pictures on 143 Studios](https://www.facebook.com/SonyPictures/posts/did-you-know-mel-robbins-named-her-company-143-studios-after-mister-rogers-belov/10156775999781931/), [Variety on the show cancellation](https://variety.com/2020/tv/news/mel-robbins-show-cancel-sony-pictures-entertainment-1203485676/), [Modern Therapy Alliance on therapist criticism](https://moderntherapyalliance.com/why-are-therapists-upset-about-mel-robbins-the-let-them-theory/), [Mel Robbins on her husband's depression](https://www.facebook.com/melrobbins/videos/i-had-no-idea-how-much-my-husband-was-struggling-with-depression/1045655386214092/)
